WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.752
This is the International Space Station.

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:09.230
It orbits the earth 16 times a day, 
250 miles up.

00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:12.719
It’s where astronauts from more than a dozen
different countries

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:15.275
have been living since the year 2000

00:00:15.275 --> 00:00:17.390
mostly Russians and Americans.

00:00:18.311 --> 00:00:22.769
And for the last 10 years, basically the only
way anyone got up or down

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:24.799
was in a Russian Soyuz.

00:00:25.465 --> 00:00:29.502
At least in space, countries like Russia and
the US depend on each other.

00:00:35.922 --> 00:00:40.500
But when Russia invaded Ukraine, the US levied
major sanctions in response

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.800
including some meant to...

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.038
"degrade their aerospace industry, 
including their space program."

00:00:46.038 --> 00:00:48.410
The head of Russia’s space agency 
responded that

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.260
perhaps they’d crash the Space Station.

00:00:52.004 --> 00:00:55.530
They even made a fake video showing Russians
leaving an American astronaut...

00:00:56.626 --> 00:01:00.150
and then detaching the Russian segment.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.060
Relations between the West and Russia are
the tensest they’ve been

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:07.174
since the International Space Station has
been in operation.

00:01:07.174 --> 00:01:09.649
So, what happens to it now?

00:01:15.345 --> 00:01:17.869
During the Cold War, the US and
the Soviet Union

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:19.615
were in the Space Race.

00:01:19.615 --> 00:01:23.772
Who could develop the technology to reach
the stars first?

00:01:23.772 --> 00:01:28.155
But space is also where the two countries
have tried to get along.

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:30.340
After the Soviet Union launched the first
satellite

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.329
and put the first man into space

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:35.209
but before the US landed on the moon

00:01:35.209 --> 00:01:38.697
there was actually talk of 
going to the moon together.

00:01:39.577 --> 00:01:42.663
"The cold reaches of the universe 
must not become

00:01:42.663 --> 00:01:45.643
the new arena of an even colder war."

00:01:45.643 --> 00:01:49.708
It actually ended up being something 
discussed in Congress.

00:01:49.708 --> 00:01:51.993
Of course, that was where it 
then fizzled out.

00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:56.272
But I think it was important 
that these discussions took place.

00:01:56.272 --> 00:02:00.883
It led to the two countries eventually collaborating
on compatible docking system.

00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:03.936
Even joining spacecrafts in 1975.

00:02:03.936 --> 00:02:06.110
The handshake was the symbol of

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.745
“We see each other as equal partners in
this."

00:02:09.745 --> 00:02:14.080
"We recognize and respect each other, at least
here in space.”

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.940
Soon after the US would begin its
Space Shuttle program

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.930
bringing astronauts and satellites into space
in reusable shuttles.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:25.470
And the Soviet Union would create
the first modular space station, Mir

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.330
where cosmonauts could live long-term.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:32.770
At the same time, the US, Japan, Canada and
several European countries

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:38.030
began planning to build their own space station
together.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.290
But after the Soviet Union fell — and the
Cold War ended

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.040
the US and Russia began peace talks.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.650
One of the first things they agreed to 
was collaboration in space.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.829
Soon US shuttle astronauts docked with Mir.

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:54.845
Russian cosmonauts joined Shuttle launches.

00:02:54.845 --> 00:02:58.660
"And liftoff on Discovery, of a bold new era
of space flight

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.613
 between the United States and Russia."

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.560
And Russia was added to the
joint station plans.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.910
"Instead of building weapons in space

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:13.370
Russian scientists will help us to build
the International Space Station."

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:16.980
The first parts of the space station went
up in 1998.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.220
It was designed to be collaborative and interdependent.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.960
It’s made up of these individual modules.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.510
Some controlled by Russia, some controlled
by the US

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.790
along with several other countries.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.585
The Russian side controls the navigation and
the US provides most of the electricity.

00:03:32.585 --> 00:03:37.454
That was a purposeful design, to sort of work
in international cooperation

00:03:37.454 --> 00:03:41.240
into the actual technicalities 
of the space station.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.740
They’ve conducted thousands of experiments
to learn about

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.070
diseases, climate change on Earth and

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.430
importantly for space exploration

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:53.060
whether humans can actually live in space
long-term.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:56.370
How does food grow, or a human body change?

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.760
The space station is sort of this 
major stepping stone

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.880
for more exploration of space

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.336
going back to the Moon, onto Mars.

00:04:04.336 --> 00:04:08.130
It's to learn about human survival in space.

00:04:09.089 --> 00:04:13.684
And international cooperation in space has
largely worked out really well.

00:04:13.684 --> 00:04:15.940
The US even retired its shuttle program

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:19.109
and became completely reliant on Russia’s
Soyuz rockets

00:04:19.109 --> 00:04:21.592
to reach the space station.

00:04:21.592 --> 00:04:22.729
But then...

00:04:23.805 --> 00:04:26.187
"Russian tanks, choppers, and troops..."

00:04:26.187 --> 00:04:28.940
"Vladimir Putin moves to annex Crimea."

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.953
In 2014 Russia invaded and annexed Crimea from Ukraine

00:04:33.953 --> 00:04:36.658
and the US imposed harsh sanctions.

00:04:36.658 --> 00:04:38.319
Just like he would again years later

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:42.039
the head of Russia’s Roscosmos
threatened to pull Soyuz access

00:04:42.039 --> 00:04:47.259
suggesting the US should get to the space
station on "a trampoline".

00:04:47.259 --> 00:04:52.050
But the two countries weren’t serious about
bringing conflict on Earth to the space station.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:55.000
At least the head of NASA then
wasn’t worried about the tweets.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.590
President Obama and President Putin put sort
of a bubble

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:59.534
around the International Space Station

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:02.792
and we continued the space diplomatic relations.

00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:07.570
That says something about the value of that
bilateral relationship.

00:05:08.627 --> 00:05:11.678
"The military has escalated its attacks on
civilians."

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:13.861
"He believes Russia is committing war crimes."

00:05:13.861 --> 00:05:15.468
"He is a war criminal."

00:05:15.468 --> 00:05:18.990
Today, the diplomatic relationship between
the US and Russia

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.919
is at the lowest point since the Cold War.

00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:26.870
But the space relationship between them remains
fairly untouched.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.529
The Russians didn’t leave the US astronaut
behind

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:31.830
and brought him back to Earth in a Soyuz.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.104
"Touchdown."

00:05:33.104 --> 00:05:36.807
"I never perceived those tweets as anything
to take seriously."

00:05:36.807 --> 00:05:41.469
"I just had too much confidence in our cooperation
to date."

00:05:41.469 --> 00:05:45.461
They even sent a new crew of cosmonauts to
the space station.

00:05:45.461 --> 00:05:48.475
"People have problems on Earth."

00:05:48.475 --> 00:05:52.629
"In orbit, we're not lying, 
we are one crew."

00:05:52.629 --> 00:05:54.930
For now the space station continues to run

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.529
mostly insulated from the 
geopolitical problems on Earth

00:05:58.529 --> 00:06:00.499
just as it was designed.

00:06:00.499 --> 00:06:03.240
Emphasis on: for now.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.000
The truth is, the space station is old.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.940
It was only designed to last until 2015.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.689
And while the countries have agreed to continue
operations several times

00:06:12.689 --> 00:06:16.716
right now that agreement only goes until 2024.

00:06:16.716 --> 00:06:19.753
The US wants to extend until 2030.

00:06:19.753 --> 00:06:22.870
Russia has yet to sign on.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:23.900
But even if they do

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.592
the two countries have different plans in
space in the future.

00:06:27.592 --> 00:06:29.410
Private companies are planning 
to build their own

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:31.639
space stations in the coming decade.

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:34.217
NASA wants to depend on those.

00:06:34.217 --> 00:06:36.983
They’re focused on developing 
the Artemis program.

00:06:36.983 --> 00:06:41.779
Going back to the moon, establishing a presence
there, and eventually, traveling to Mars.

00:06:41.779 --> 00:06:44.229
In collaboration with other countries.

00:06:44.229 --> 00:06:46.787
With two notable exceptions.

00:06:46.787 --> 00:06:50.560
Russia is currently planning and building
its own space station.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.796
So is China, who plans to finish their station,
already in orbit, this year.

00:06:54.796 --> 00:06:57.139
They’re also planning a mission to the moon.

00:06:57.139 --> 00:07:00.637
I think we're still going to see — we must
see — international cooperation in space.

00:07:00.637 --> 00:07:06.672
But the question is just which countries will
cooperate with which other countries.

00:07:06.672 --> 00:07:11.805
As for the International Space Station, if
Russia doesn’t participate after 2024

00:07:11.805 --> 00:07:16.639
things they currently control, like navigation, could
be designed on the US side.

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:19.832
Likely with the help of private companies
like SpaceX.

00:07:19.832 --> 00:07:23.750
SpaceX is already how many astronauts get
to the space station.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:27.756
Their first mission in 2020 ended the dependence
on Russia’s Soyuz.

00:07:27.756 --> 00:07:29.274
"The trampoline is working!"

00:07:35.557 --> 00:07:36.423
"It’s an inside joke."

00:07:36.423 --> 00:07:37.622
"Inside joke here."

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:44.375
The International Space Station is really
one of the best examples 

00:07:44.375 --> 00:07:46.980
in history of human cooperation.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.599
It can represent the best of humanity.

00:07:50.599 --> 00:07:53.969
The war in Ukraine may hasten the end of the
space station.

00:07:53.969 --> 00:07:57.129
But that end was already planned.

00:07:57.129 --> 00:07:58.409
What it really threatens

00:07:58.409 --> 00:08:02.883
is the future of these countries finding 
common ground in space.

00:08:02.883 --> 00:08:05.099
"My hope is that these types of connections

00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:08.379
can be maintained and serve as a path forward

00:08:08.379 --> 00:08:13.643
to try and find that common ground
that we need so desperately to find peace."

